    "title": "Chain rule differentiation",
    "difficulty": 1,
    "problem": "Differentiate $y = \\sin^{-1}(3x^2)$ and find $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ at $x = \\dfrac{1}{3}$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "step": 1,
        "instruction": "Apply the chain rule with $u = 3x^2$, using $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\sin^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$.",
        "working": "Let $u = 3x^2$, so $\\dfrac{du}{dx} = 6x$. $$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - (3x^2)^2}} \\cdot 6x = \\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{1 - 9x^4}}$$"
      },
      {
        "step": 2,
        "instruction": "Evaluate at $x = \\dfrac{1}{3}$.",
        "working": "$$\\frac{dy}{dx}\\bigg|_{x=1/3} = \\frac{6 \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}}{\\sqrt{1 - 9 \\cdot \\frac{1}{81}}} = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{9}}} = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}}} = \\frac{2}{\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}} = \\frac{2 \\cdot 3}{2\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}$$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$\\dfrac{dy}{dx} = \\dfrac{6x}{\\sqrt{1-9x^4}}$, and $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}\\Big|_{x=1/3} = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}$."
  },
