    "difficulty": 2,
    "problem": "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^3 \\frac{1}{9 + x^2}\\,dx$.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "step": 1,
        "instruction": "Match the integrand to the standard form $\\int \\dfrac{1}{a^2+x^2}\\,dx = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\dfrac{x}{a}\\right) + C$.",
        "working": "Write $9 + x^2 = 3^2 + x^2$, so $a = 3$. $$\\int \\frac{1}{a^2 + x^2}\\,dx = \\frac{1}{a}\\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{x}{a}\\right) + C = \\frac{1}{3}\\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right) + C$$"
      },
      {
        "step": 2,
        "instruction": "Apply the limits.",
        "working": "$$\\int_0^3 \\frac{1}{9+x^2}\\,dx = \\left[\\frac{1}{3}\\tan^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\right]_0^3 = \\frac{1}{3}\\tan^{-1}(1) - \\frac{1}{3}\\tan^{-1}(0)$$ $$= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\frac{\\pi}{4} - 0 = \\frac{\\pi}{12}$$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$\\displaystyle\\int_0^3 \\frac{1}{9+x^2}\\,dx = \\frac{\\pi}{12}$"
  },
