        "instruction": "Apply the product rule, using $\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\sin^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$.",
        "working": "$$g'(x) = 2x \\cdot \\sin^{-1}(x) + x^2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$$"
      },
      {
        "step": 2,
        "instruction": "Evaluate at $x = 0$.",
        "working": "$$g'(0) = 2(0)\\sin^{-1}(0) + \\frac{0^2}{\\sqrt{1-0}} = 0 + 0 = 0$$"
      },
      {
        "step": 3,
        "instruction": "Evaluate at $x = \\dfrac{1}{2}$.",
        "working": "$$g'\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\sin^{-1}\\!\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) + \\frac{(1/2)^2}{\\sqrt{1 - 1/4}}$$ $$= 1 \\cdot \\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\frac{1/4}{\\sqrt{3/4}} = \\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\frac{1/4}{\\sqrt{3}/2} = \\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{\\pi}{6} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}$$"
      }
    ],
    "answer": "$g'(x) = 2x\\sin^{-1}(x) + \\dfrac{x^2}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$. $g'(0) = 0$ and $g'\\!\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}$."
  }
